honor-fmbp-config — input, device and power quirks for the HONOR MagicBook Pro 14
================================================================================

Battery charge limit
--------------------
The EC enforces a charge limit only for three OEM preset ranges (arbitrary pairs
are ignored) and does not remember it across reboots, so honor-battery-thresholds
.service re-applies it at boot. It ships set to the balanced 70–90% preset.

  Presets (START END):  40 70  (max lifespan) / 70 90 (balanced) / 95 100 (max capacity)
  Change it:            edit /etc/default/honor-fmbp-battery, then
                        sudo systemctl restart honor-battery-thresholds.service
  Check it:            cat /sys/devices/platform/huawei-wmi/charge_control_thresholds

Requires BIOS >= 1.16 (earlier firmware exposes the sysfs node but does not enforce it).

Battery Quick Settings panel (optional)
---------------------------------------
For a GNOME Quick Settings toggle between the three presets, install the
"Battery Health Charging" extension (by maniacx). It is third-party, so it is not
shipped here — install it yourself:

  1. sudo apt install gnome-shell-extension-manager   (pulled in by the metapackage)
  2. Open "Extension Manager" -> Browse -> search "Battery Health Charging" -> Install.
  3. Switch to the "Installed" tab and make sure its toggle is ON. Installing does
     not always enable it, and a system reinstall or home restore can leave it
     present but disabled (so the Quick Settings panel silently never appears).
     Equivalently from a terminal:
         gnome-extensions enable Battery-Health-Charging@maniacx.github.com
  4. Log out and back in if the toggle still does not appear (gnome-shell loads
     extensions at session start).

honor-fmbp-config pre-seeds this extension's presets (via a dconf system default) to
match the ranges huawei-wmi enforces, so its panel is correct out of the box. These
are defaults only — you can still change them in the extension's preferences. On KDE
Plasma the same charge limit is available natively in Power Management (no extension).

Keyboard backlight
------------------
The keyboard backlight (from honor-fmbp-kbdlight-dkms) appears as a native GNOME 45+
Quick Settings brightness control — no extension needed. gnome-shell enumerates
keyboard backlights at startup, so after first install you may need to log out and
back in for the control to show up.

